"""One-off: second trim of the longest skill descriptions. Run from the repo root.
Keeps the trigger situations and the skip pointers between neighbouring skills,
drops sentences that describe how the skill works (those live in the skill body)."""
import re

NEW = {
"security-hardening": "Locks down how a system is configured and run. Use for hardening a cloud account, servers, containers, networks, databases, CI/CD pipelines and secrets, preparing for a pen test, audit or security questionnaire, fixing findings such as open ports or missing headers, MFA and least privilege, and public buckets. Fixes the riskiest exposure first in small steps. Skip for reviewing application code (security-review), threat modelling a feature (security-prompter) and a live attack (incident-response).",
"privacy-compliance": "Finds and fixes privacy and data-protection gaps. Use for GDPR, CCPA, HIPAA, COPPA, what personal data we store, consent and cookie banners, privacy policies, deleting or exporting a user's data, retention, sending data to vendors or AI providers, DPAs, breaches and children's data. Not legal advice and never certifies compliance. Skip for code vulnerabilities (security-review) and threat modelling a feature (security-prompter).",
"mobile-ci": "Sets up build, test, signing and distribution pipelines for iOS and Android apps. Use for mobile CI/CD, Fastlane, EAS, Bitrise, GitHub Actions for mobile, certificates, provisioning profiles, keystores, TestFlight and Google Play uploads, build numbers, flaky emulator tests, macOS runner cost and crash symbols. Skip for web and server pipelines (release-engineering) and store policy or app design (mobile-app-work).",
"library-cli-work": "Builds and publishes libraries, packages and command-line tools that others install. Use for publishing to PyPI, npm or crates.io, a library's public surface, semantic versioning, CLI flags and exit codes, supported versions, packaging and release steps. Skip for HTTP or GraphQL services (api-design) and deploying your own app (release-engineering).",
"documentation": "Plans, audits and maintains a project's documentation. Use when docs are out of date or a mess, new people take too long to get started, deciding what docs a project needs, setting up a docs site (MkDocs, Docusaurus, Sphinx), API reference, decision records, runbooks, or keeping docs in sync with code. Skip for polishing one document's wording (plain-writing) and writing a repo's CLAUDE.md (codebase-onboarding).",
"design-reference": "Turns a visual reference into a written design guide to build from. Use for make it look like this site, match our brand, a screenshot or mockup to follow, creating a design system, writing a DESIGN.md or extracting styles. Copies no logos, images, text or code. Skip when there is no reference and the user wants colours, type or layout chosen (frontend-design).",
"frontend-design": "Makes a user interface look deliberately designed instead of generic. Use before building or restyling any page, component or screen, for landing pages and dashboards, or when output looks like a default template. Fixes a design brief first (audience, tone, type, colour, spacing, layout), then builds from tokens; for landing pages it also sets offer, structure and copy. Skip for backend work and tiny copy or colour tweaks.",
"llm-app-work": "Builds applications that call a language model (chatbots, RAG, agents with tools, extraction) so they are tested, safe and affordable. Use for adding an AI feature, prompts, structured output, embeddings, prompt injection, hallucinations, evaluating answers, token cost and latency, and model upgrades. Builds an eval set first. Skip for training your own model (data-ml-work) and ordinary API design (api-design).",
"requirements-interview": "Gets missing requirements from the user before anything is built. Use when a request is vague or underspecified (add notifications, build me an app for X), when the user says interview me or what do you need to know, and before a new app, large feature, migration or architecture change. Asks only what Claude cannot look up or default. Skip when the request is already specific and small.",
"playwright-ui-tests": "Builds and maintains a Playwright end-to-end test suite for a web UI. Use for adding UI tests, setting up Playwright, testing critical flows, visual regression tests, running UI tests in CI and flaky Playwright tests. Skip for one-off visual checks (browser-verify) and unit tests (test-strategy).",
"release-engineering": "Designs and runs safe releases and deployment pipelines. Use for setting up CI/CD, release processes, shipping to production, cutting a release, rollback, versioning, release notes, or deploys that are manual, risky or have broken production. Covers rollout with abort criteria and rollback including database changes. Never deploys, tags or publishes without confirmation. Skip for local-only scripts.",
"change-review": "Reviews a code change (pull request, diff, patch or the user's own branch) and returns a verdict with ranked, actionable comments. Use for review this PR, look over this diff, is this safe to merge, or feedback on a change. Starts from what the change is meant to do and where it can hurt most. Skip for security-only audits of a whole app and for writing the change itself.",
"web-quality": "Checks a web page or app for accessibility, performance and responsive problems and fixes them. Use for is it accessible, audit the page, it loads slowly, Lighthouse, works on mobile, keyboard navigation, screen reader, or before launching a frontend. Skip for backend performance and for formal WCAG audits or VPATs (accessibility-review).",
"dev-orchestrator": "Runs a task as a small team when it is worth it. You act as manager and spawn specialist subagents to propose, critique, settle the best approach, then implement and verify. Use for hard-to-reverse design choices or real risk, such as use a team of agents, get several opinions then build, large features and migrations. Skip for small or clear tasks, where one agent is cheaper.",
"test-strategy": "Decides what to test and writes tests that catch real bugs. Use for what should I test, write tests for this, flaky or slow tests, coverage, is this test any good, setting up testing, or code about to change with weak tests. Chooses tests by risk and checks a test would fail when the code is wrong. Skip when the only task is fixing one failing test (honest-fixes).",
"skill-capture": "Spots repeated work and turns it into a reusable skill. Use when the user does the same thing again and again, asks to make this a skill, automate a workflow, save this for next time or teach Claude how we do X, or when the same procedure or correction comes up a second time. Propose it and ask before creating anything. Skip one-off tasks and anything an existing skill covers.",
"browser-verify": "Drives a real browser with Playwright to check that a web UI works and looks right. Use for test the UI, does it work in the browser, take a screenshot, check the login flow, works on my machine but the page is broken, or after a frontend change worth seeing. Captures screenshots and console errors. Skip for API or unit-level work; for a lasting suite use playwright-ui-tests.",
"dev-lifecycle": "The entry point for building or changing software end to end. Use when the user asks to build, ship, fix, upgrade, migrate or rebuild something and it is unclear where to start, or says take this from idea to production, what is the process, or do this properly. Routes through discover, design, build, verify, review, ship and learn and names the skill for each phase. Skip for small edits and questions.",
"git-hygiene": "Keeps git history clean and recovers from git mistakes without losing work. Use for squashing or rewriting commits, rebase versus merge, undoing a commit or push, lost changes, a pushed secret or large file, force pushes and branching rules. Backs up before any history change and never force-pushes a shared branch without a named yes. Skip for deploy pipelines (release-engineering) and reviewing a change (change-review).",
}

before = after = 0
for name, desc in NEW.items():
    assert ': ' not in desc, name
    p = f'skills/{name}/SKILL.md'
    s = open(p, encoding='utf-8').read()
    old = re.search(r'^description:\s*(.*)$', s, re.M).group(1)
    s2, n = re.subn(r'^description:.*$', 'description: ' + desc.replace('\\', '\\\\'), s, count=1, flags=re.M)
    assert n == 1, name
    open(p, 'w', encoding='utf-8').write(s2)
    before += len(old); after += len(desc)
print('updated', len(NEW), 'chars', before, '->', after)
